## Solution

**Key observation — recognize the integrand as a total derivative.**

Consider the derivative of $e^{nx}\tan^n x$:

$$\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[e^{nx}\tan^n x\right] = n\,e^{nx}\tan^n x + e^{nx}\cdot n\tan^{n-1}x\,\sec^2 x.$$

Factor out $n\,e^{nx}\tan^{n-1}x$:

$$\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[e^{nx}\tan^n x\right] = n\,e^{nx}\tan^{n-1}x\bigl(\tan x + \sec^2 x\bigr).$$

Using $\sec^2 x = 1+\tan^2 x$, the bracket becomes

$$\tan x + 1 + \tan^2 x = 1 + \tan x + \tan^2 x.$$

Therefore

$$\frac{d}{dx}\!\left[e^{nx}\tan^n x\right] = n\,e^{nx}\tan^{n-1}x\bigl(1+\tan x+\tan^2 x\bigr) = n\,e^{nx}\bigl(\tan^{n-1}x+\tan^n x+\tan^{n+1}x\bigr).$$

**Evaluate $I_n$ by the Fundamental Theorem.**

Since the integrand of $I_n$ is exactly $\frac{1}{n}\frac{d}{dx}[e^{nx}\tan^n x]$,

$$I_n = \frac{1}{n}\Bigl[e^{nx}\tan^n x\Bigr]_{0}^{\pi/4}.$$

At $x=\pi/4$: $\tan(\pi/4)=1$, so $e^{nx}\tan^n x\big|_{x=\pi/4}=e^{n\pi/4}$.
At $x=0$: $\tan 0 = 0$, so $e^{nx}\tan^n x\big|_{x=0}=0$ (for $n\ge 1$).

Hence

$$I_n = \frac{e^{n\pi/4}}{n}, \qquad\text{so}\qquad n\,I_n = e^{n\pi/4}.$$

**Compute the limit.**

$$\sqrt[n^2]{\,nI_n\,} = \left(e^{n\pi/4}\right)^{1/n^2} = e^{\,\pi/(4n)}.$$

Thus

$$n\!\left(\sqrt[n^2]{nI_n}-1\right) = n\!\left(e^{\,\pi/(4n)}-1\right).$$

Using $e^t - 1 \sim t$ as $t\to 0$ with $t = \frac{\pi}{4n}$,

$$n\!\left(e^{\,\pi/(4n)}-1\right) \;\sim\; n\cdot\frac{\pi}{4n} = \frac{\pi}{4}.$$

Therefore

$$\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty} n\!\left(\sqrt[n^2]{\,nI_n\,}-1\right) = \frac{\pi}{4}}.$$

